  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nConfidence remains high that quiet and dry weather will persist\nthrough Wednesday afternoon under high pressure. Temperatures\nwill trend above average through tomorrow with southerly winds \nahead of an approaching disturbance.\n\nModels continue to depict a large-scale trough tracking over the\nGreat Lakes early Wednesday into Thursday. This system will \nweaken the ridge over the central CONUS as it moves eastward\noffshore the Mid-Atlantic coastline. This will allow for more \nactive weather to develop across the region.\n\nWith the low-level moisture advection (dew points climbing into\nthe upper-60s/low-70s) and surface temperatures in the mid to \nupper-80s, heat index values could approach 90 degrees by late \nWednesday afternoon in a few areas and cause heat stress to \nlocal communities. Parts of eastern Ohio could have a 30%-50% \nfor heat indices to exceed 90 degrees.\n\nBetween 7pm to 9pm Wednesday, precipitation chances will \nincrease just south of Lake Erie with an approaching trough\nfollowed by a cold front. This will move southeastward overnight\nand be into northern WV by Thursday morning. The probability of\nstrong to severe storms rose to 15%-29% for parts of \nnorthwestern PA and eastern Ohio where the greatest probability\nfor 750 J/kg of CAPE and 30kts of shear will be along with\nnotable curvature in 0-3km hodographs. The main threat with \nthese storms will be wind, though an isolated tornado could\ndevelop in storms with a subtle right veer from storm motion. \nSunset appears to significantly reduce the instability thus \nreducing the chance for southwestern PA and northern WV to \nexperience severe weather.\n\nThe prolonged passage of the trough may support periods of\nmoderate to heavy rainfall totals on Thursday, potentially\nresulting in isolated flooding near creeks and streams. Some \nareas could receive 1 to 1.5 inches of rainfall during this \nperiod. Currently, there is low confidence with models not\nagreeing on the location of the heaviest precipitation with some  \nshowing an area from southern Ohio extending northeastward into\ncentral PA while others focusing more over western Ohio and \nsouthern Indiana.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nMachine-learning models suggest that after a brief period of \nridging, Friday through Saturday, our large-scale weather \npattern will transition to a more zonal flow. As a result, this  \nwill allow disturbances forming upstream (near the Rockies) to \ntrack into our area. This pattern could create a heavy rain \nthreat and/or a severe weather threat early next week and will\nneed to be monitored.",
